{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And every sin offering whose blood is brought</b> — that is, if he brought the blood of an outer sin offering inside [the Sanctuary], it is disqualified. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 6:23). According to this opinion, what the verse says, \"to make atonement in the Holy,\" is not literal, for this offering does not make atonement, since it has been disqualified by this act of bringing [the blood inside], and it is because of this disqualification that it is burned. Rather, Scripture says \"to make atonement\" in the sense that if he brought it inside with the intention of making atonement — that he sprinkles from it there like the procedure of an inner sin offering — even though it did not effect atonement, that is, he did not sprinkle its blood at all, it is disqualified from the moment of entry and must be burned. And it is possible that if he brought it inside with the intention of not sprinkling from it at all, it remains valid. And according to the opinion of Rabbi Shimon (Zevahim 82a), it is not disqualified unless he made atonement with its blood, that is, he performed sprinklings from it like the procedure of an inner sin offering; therefore Scripture says \"to make atonement\" — not that it did make atonement, but that he brought its blood to make atonement with it and did make atonement according to his intention. And the meaning of \"inside\" is that he brought the blood into the Hekhal. And likewise if he brought the blood of the bull of the anointed priest [for a communal sin offering involving] the concealment of a matter, and of the goats [offered for] idolatry, both for the outer [Sanctuary] and for the inner [Sanctuary] — all of these, if he brought [their blood] inside from their proper places, they are disqualified. And Scripture says \"from its blood\" to teach that even a portion of its blood disqualifies it: if he received its blood in two cups and brought one of them inside, it is disqualified (there [in Zevahim]). And according to the plain sense of Scripture, the verse speaks only of the inner sin offerings, whose burning He has already commanded, and it comes here to establish a negative prohibition against eating them, for this section completes the laws of the sin offerings. And this is the midrash of Rabbi Yosi the Galilean in Torat Kohanim (chapter 8, halakhah 8) and in Zevahim (there):"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The Hebrew 'אע\"פ שלא כפר בה' is rendered 'even though it did not effect atonement'; the phrase כלומר שלא הזה מדמהו כלום is Ramban's parenthetical gloss explaining in what sense it failed to atone."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:22.684576154Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}